    slug: 'texas-rubinsky-roofing-insurance-claim-scam',
    description: `Rubinsky Roofing LLC, a Dallas-Fort Worth-area contractor owned by Gilad Rubinsky, used aggressive door-to-door and phone sales tactics to pressure North Texas homeowners into signing roofing contracts tied to storm-damage insurance claims, then collected large payments — including insurance checks the company had customers sign over directly — and left the work delayed for months, unfinished, or never started at all. Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton sued the company and Rubinsky individually in Dallas County in May 2026, alleging violations of the Texas Deceptive Trade Practices Act and citing roughly $500,000 in combined consumer losses, including one homeowner who paid over $24,000 for a roof replacement that was never completed and another whose $10,000 insurance check produced no work for six months. The lawsuit specifically alleges the company preyed on elderly Texans, with sales representatives in at least one case repeatedly pressuring an older consumer to pursue a roof-damage insurance claim the homeowner did not believe was needed. The Better Business Bureau had already revoked Rubinsky Roofing's accreditation in January 2026 after a surge of complaints. Paxton's office is seeking temporary and permanent injunctions barring the company from continuing the practice, along with consumer restitution and civil penalties. Homeowners approached about storm-damage roof repair are advised to get a written, itemized contract before any insurance check is signed over, to avoid handing an insurance payout directly to the contractor before work is inspected and completed, and to check a roofing company's complaint history and BBB accreditation before hiring.`,
